 ## Background
-The original NeRF paper [S2] introduced the basic MLP architecture. Subsequent research has expanded this into a diverse family of architectures to address specific challenges such as single-view synthesis [S1, unofficial][S2], real-time rendering [S1, unofficial], geometric accuracy [S1, unofficial][S2, S3], and large-scale scenes [S1, unofficial][S3, S6].
+The original NeRF architecture [S2] established the baseline of using an MLP to map 3D coordinates and view directions to density and color. Subsequent research has diversified the architecture to handle specific challenges:
+*   **Efficiency:** Methods like [AdaNeRF](6195-adanerf.md) and [MIMO-NeRF](10928-mimo-nerf.md) modify the network's input/output structure to reduce computational load.
+*   **Generalization/Sparse Views:** [SinNeRF](6199-sinnerf.md), [NeRDi](9112-nerdi.md), and [DiffusioNeRF](9572-generative-and-style-based-models.md) integrate external priors (depth, diffusion) to overcome the lack of multi-view constraints.
+*   **Semantic/Instance Understanding:** [NeRF-RPN](8663-nerf-rpn.md) and [Instance-NeRF](10611-instance-nerf.md) extend the output to include bounding boxes and instance labels.
+*   **Physical Properties:** [IRON](10251-iron.md) and [Appearance and Lighting Decomposition](11061-appearance-and-lighting-decomposition.md) separate geometry, material, and lighting for inverse rendering tasks.
+*   **Dynamic/Deformable Scenes:** [Deformable Radiance Fields](9572-generative-and-style-based-models.md) use displacement fields to model time-varying geometry.
 
